  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 133 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nMonday...Shortwave ridging builds in ahead of the next trough \nzooming across the Northern Plains, keeping conditions quiet and \nmild on Monday as the elongated high pressure camps over our \narea. This will not last long though as the next cold front over\nthe Dakotas and Minnesota inches towards Iowa.\n\nTuesday-Thursday...Despite the shortwave trough over the Northern \nPlain lifting into south-central Canada on Tuesday, a longwave \ntrough remains in place which supports the continued propagation of \nthe cold front into Iowa. PoPs return mostly supported by diffluent \nflow aloft and the front, but dry air in the mid-levels should limit \ncoverage (10-20%) and precipitation amounts. As the longwave\nslowly shifts eastward on Wednesday, a deepening low pressure \nover the South Plains will support some mid-level F-gen over \nIowa north of the slowing cold front, keeping PoPs (30-50%) and \ncloud cover around. By Thursday, the longwave trough finally \nreaches the Upper and Middle Mississippi River Valley, pushing \nthe cold front completely through the area and bumping down PoPs\n(20-30%). Temperatures during this period are likely (60-90%) \nto be seasonable with highs in the low-to-mid 80s and lows in \nthe low-to-mid 60s.\n\nFriday...There is a lot of uncertainty on rain chances on Friday as \nthe longwave trough still sits over the area with shortwaves likely \nembedded. The presence of a stationary front south of the CWA could \nalso act as a lifting mechanism, but pockets of dry air aloft due to \nthe northwesterly flow should hopefully keep PoPs and precipitation \namounts on the lower side.",
